{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0035","fact_refs":[0],"question":"How many tweet-inherent features did the researchers analyze for country-level classification of tweets?","answer":"They analyzed eight tweet-inherent features."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0035","fact_refs":[1],"question":"When were the two tweet datasets used in this study collected?","answer":"They were collected a year apart, in October 2014 and October 2015."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0035","fact_refs":[2],"question":"How many countries were used in the full classification experiment, and what was the size of the reduced subset?","answer":"The full experiment covered all 217 countries in the datasets, with a reduced subset of the top 25 countries by tweet volume."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0035","fact_refs":[3],"question":"How much improvement can combining tweet content and metadata provide over using a single feature for country classification?","answer":"An appropriate combination of tweet content and metadata can lead to improvements of between 20% and 50%."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0035","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How many country-coded tweets do the two public datasets contain in total?","answer":"The two datasets together contain over 5 million country-coded tweets."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0035","fact_refs":[5],"question":"For which types of countries does the classification become slightly more difficult?","answer":"Classification difficulty increases slightly for countries with multiple commonalities, especially English and Spanish speaking countries."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0036","statement":"The proposed spammer classification approach is based on Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA), a topic model.","source_span":"a novel spammer classification approach based on Latent Dirichlet Allocation(LDA), a topic model"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0036","statement":"The method was tested on one benchmark dataset (Honeypot) and one self-collected dataset (Weibo).","source_span":"Tested on one benchmark dataset and one self-collected dataset"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0036","statement":"Two topic-based features are extracted: the Local Outlier Standard Score (LOSS) and the Global Outlier Standard Score (GOSS).","source_span":"the Local Outlier Standard Score (LOSS) which captures the user's interests on different topics and the Global Outlier Standard Score (GOSS) which reveals the user's interests on specific topic in comparison with other users'"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0036","statement":"Microblogging services like Twitter and Weibo allow users to post messages up to 140 characters.","source_span":"allows users to post messages up to 140 characters"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0036","statement":"Social spammers are divided into two categories: content polluters and fake accounts.","source_span":"social spammers can be divided into two categories. One is content polluters... The other is fake accounts"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0036","statement":"On the Honeypot dataset, spammers show a narrower topic distribution than legitimate users because there are more content polluters than fake accounts.","source_span":"spammers have a narrower shape of distribution... than that of legitimate users. This is because there are more content polluters than fake accounts"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0036","statement":"On the Weibo dataset, fake accounts interested in a wide range of topics make up a large proportion of spammers, giving a flatter topic distribution than legitimate users.","source_span":"fake accounts who are interested in different topics take large proportion of spammers. Their distribution is more flat (i.e. red bars) than that of the legitimate users"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0036","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What topic model underlies the proposed spammer classification approach?","answer":"The approach is based on Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA), a topic model."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0036","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What are the two topic-based features extracted for spammer classification, and what does each capture?","answer":"LOSS (Local Outlier Standard Score) captures a user's interests across different topics, while GOSS (Global Outlier Standard Score) reveals a user's interest in a specific topic compared to other users."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0036","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What is the maximum message length allowed on microblogging platforms like Twitter and Weibo, according to the paper?","answer":"Messages can be up to 140 characters long."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0036","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Into what two categories are social spammers divided in this paper?","answer":"They are divided into content polluters and fake accounts."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0036","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Which two datasets were used to test the proposed spammer detection method?","answer":"The benchmark Honeypot dataset and a self-collected Weibo dataset."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0036","fact_refs":[6],"question":"Why does the Weibo dataset show a flatter spammer topic distribution than the Honeypot dataset?","answer":"Because fake accounts, which are interested in a wide range of topics due to random copying and retweeting, make up a large proportion of spammers in the Weibo dataset."}
